{"subjectId":"sql","id":"aggregate-functions","title":"Aggregate Functions","group":"Functions & Aggregation","order":23,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Aggregate functions collapse many rows into one value: how many, how much, the average, the extremes.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Returns</th><th>NULLs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>COUNT(*)</code></td><td>Number of rows</td><td>Counted</td></tr><tr><td><code>COUNT(col)</code></td><td>Number of non-null values</td><td>Ignored</td></tr><tr><td><code>COUNT(DISTINCT col)</code></td><td>Number of different non-null values</td><td>Ignored</td></tr><tr><td><code>SUM(col)</code></td><td>Total</td><td>Ignored (all <code>NULL</code> → <code>NULL</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>AVG(col)</code></td><td>Mean = <code>SUM / COUNT(col)</code></td><td>Ignored</td></tr><tr><td><code>MIN(col)</code> / <code>MAX(col)</code></td><td>Smallest / largest (also for text and dates)</td><td>Ignored</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT COUNT(*)                 AS employees,\n       COUNT(email)             AS with_email,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT dept_id)  AS departments,\n       SUM(salary)              AS payroll,\n       AVG(salary)              AS avg_salary,\n       MIN(hire_date)           AS first_hire,\n       MAX(name)                AS last_name_alpha\nFROM employees;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>employees | with_email | departments | payroll | avg_salary | first_hire | last_name_alpha\n----------+------------+-------------+---------+------------+------------+----------------\n10        | 8          | 3           | 937000  | 93700.0    | 2015-03-01 | Vikram</code></pre></figure>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-question-mark\"></i>NULLs change averages</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>CREATE TABLE ratings (product TEXT, stars INTEGER);\nINSERT INTO ratings VALUES ('A', 5), ('A', 3), ('A', NULL), ('B', NULL);\n\nSELECT product,\n       COUNT(*)              AS rows_,\n       COUNT(stars)          AS rated,\n       AVG(stars)            AS avg_ignoring_null,\n       AVG(COALESCE(stars, 0)) AS avg_null_as_zero,\n       SUM(stars)            AS total\nFROM ratings\nGROUP BY product\nORDER BY product;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>product | rows_ | rated | avg_ignoring_null | avg_null_as_zero   | total\n--------+-------+-------+-------------------+--------------------+------\nA       | 3     | 2     | 4.0               | 2.6666666666666665 | 8\nB       | 1     | 0     | NULL              | 0.0                | NULL</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Decide what <code>NULL</code> means for your metric. Product B shows the other surprise: <code>SUM</code> over only <code>NULL</code>s is <code>NULL</code>, not <code>0</code> — wrap it: <code>COALESCE(SUM(x), 0)</code>.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-circle-dashed\"></i>Aggregates over an empty set</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT COUNT(*) AS n, SUM(salary) AS total, MAX(salary) AS top\nFROM employees\nWHERE dept_id = 4;             -- Marketing has nobody</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>n | total | top\n--+-------+-----\n0 | NULL  | NULL</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Without <code>GROUP BY</code>, an aggregate query always returns <strong>exactly one row</strong> — even when no rows matched. With <code>GROUP BY</code>, no matching rows means no groups and zero result rows.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-filter\"></i>Conditional aggregation</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT SUM(CASE WHEN category = 'Electronics' THEN price END) AS electronics_value,\n       AVG(CASE WHEN category = 'Furniture'   THEN price END) AS avg_furniture,\n       COUNT(CASE WHEN price &lt; 100 THEN 1 END)                 AS cheap_items\nFROM products;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>electronics_value | avg_furniture | cheap_items\n------------------+---------------+------------\n69500             | 6250.0        | 2</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>You cannot nest aggregates (<code>MAX(AVG(salary))</code>) in one level in most engines. Aggregate in a subquery or CTE first, then aggregate the result.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between COUNT(*), COUNT(1) and COUNT(column)?</summary><div><p><code>COUNT(*)</code> and <code>COUNT(1)</code> both count rows and perform the same in modern engines. <code>COUNT(column)</code> counts rows where that column is <strong>not</strong> <code>NULL</code>. <code>COUNT(DISTINCT column)</code> counts distinct non-null values.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Does AVG include NULL values?</summary><div><p>No. <code>AVG(x)</code> = <code>SUM(x) / COUNT(x)</code> over non-null values only. To treat missing as zero, use <code>AVG(COALESCE(x, 0))</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}